  "services": {
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "“Now That’s What I Call An Era: Ballroom Blitz 1967★1976” is a broad, exuberant survey of British and international pop-rock’s transition from late-’60s bubblegum and novelty pop to the glitter, glam, and chart-focused rock of the mid-’70s. The collection brings together instantly recognizable hits including Sweet’s “Ballroom Blitz,” Mud’s “Dyna-Mite,” Suzi Quatro’s “Can the Can,” Wizzard’s “See My Baby Jive,” Elton John’s “Crocodile Rock,” Bay City Rollers’ “Shang-a-Lang,” Roxy Music’s “Virginia Plain,” and Sparks’ “This Town Ain’t Big Enough for Both of Us.”\n\nIts appeal lies in the vivid contrast between styles: psychedelic-tinged pop, orchestral glam, teenage-idol anthems, power-pop, novelty songs, and polished singer-songwriter hits share space across the program. Alongside major stars, the compilation preserves the era’s lighter and more eccentric chart culture through tracks such as “Sugar Sugar,” “Simon Says,” “Yummy Yummy Yummy,” “Rockin’ Robin,” “Chirpy Chirpy Cheep Cheep,” and “Remember You’re a Womble.” Issued as a Sony/EMI-era various-artists compilation in expanded 4CD and vinyl editions, it functions both as an accessible nostalgia set and as a colorful document of how pop spectacle and memorable hooks dominated the singles market between 1967 and 1976.",
      "generated_at": "2026-11-19T19:38:45.979687",
      "model": "sonar",
      "source": "perplexity"
    }
